## What Problem This Solves

Fixes three test fixtures that stopped before their original assertions after runtime-process helper resolution moved to maintained manifest and URL owners. The dependency-free Docker package harness omitted new relative source dependencies, the MXC backend suite replaced the complete process-runtime module with a one-export mock, and the standalone metadata VM still modeled the earlier resolver inputs and source composition.

## User Impact

There is no user-visible production change. Packaging startup coverage, Windows MXC safeguards, and standalone embedded-image controls can exercise the current runtime-process contracts again without changing helper behavior, dependencies, timeouts, skips, or platform policy.

The standalone fixture continues to distinguish compiler-owned embedded modules from ordinary disk entries. Embedded resolvers remain usable without disk access, while missing entries, unsafe permissions, path escapes, and entrypoint symlinks leaving the plugin root retain their rejection coverage.

## Why This Change Was Made

The fixtures now consume the same owners as their production callers:

- the copied-source packaging harness includes the runtime dependency resolver and manifest required by `npm-command.ts`;
- the MXC test keeps the real process-runtime exports and overrides only subprocess execution;
- the standalone VM composes the runtime manifest, package/plugin URL resolvers, and runtime argv owner, then supplies real embedded and disk fixtures for their respective contracts.

This keeps the repair at the stale caller boundary. It does not add another resolver, duplicate manifest data, or introduce a test-only production seam.

## Evidence

Hosted CI at the unchanged failing source identified the three pre-assertion failures in Windows packaging, Windows MXC, and standalone metadata jobs. On the final candidate tree `1dd523efe9181a0d7000f45e7dc51b6398eef14a`, remote focused validation produced:

- standalone metadata: 51/51 cases passed;
- Docker packaging owner: 64 passed, with three unrelated platform skips;
- MXC owner on Linux: one passed, with 40 Windows-only cases correctly left for the hosted Windows lane;
- changed-file format and lint passed;
- affected `extensions-test`, `test-root-scripts`, and `test-root-e2e` type graphs passed.

Actual Windows execution, broad exact-head CI, automated review, and independent engineering review are pending on this early non-draft PR. Linux skips are not treated as Windows proof.

## What Problem This Solves

ElevenLabs users can configure pronunciation dictionaries, but OpenClaw currently omits them from TTS requests, so those dictionary rules do not affect generated speech.

Fixes #182966

## Why This Change Was Made

The provider now validates and forwards up to three ordered dictionary ID and version pairs in the vendor's request format for both buffered and streaming synthesis. An empty list omits the field, and the input text stays unchanged.

## User Impact

Users can apply existing ElevenLabs pronunciation dictionaries through `tts.providers.elevenlabs.pronunciationDictionaryLocators`.

## Evidence

- `pnpm exec vitest run extensions/elevenlabs/speech-provider.test.ts`: 16 tests passed.
- Proof command: `pnpm exec tsx production-boundary-proof.mjs` from the repository root; the complete harness source follows below.
- The real `buildElevenLabsSpeechProvider().synthesize` and `.streamSynthesize` paths returned 3 audio bytes each. The **before-fix base** `6c33f5ff5ee0b7d438238edfdebb2762acd72503` omitted locators; the exact current head `c276373b2d091d6bd26c5c2f5e547dd8e09bd696` sends both in order. Both preserved `Please pronounce OpenClaw and ElevenLabs.` The current-head empty-list negative control omitted the field. A four-entry list was rejected before network I/O, with the loopback request count unchanged at 3. The placeholder API key was sent only to loopback; no live ElevenLabs request was made.
- `pnpm exec vitest run extensions/elevenlabs/speech-runtime.contract.test.ts`: 4 tests passed. It uses the public `openclaw/plugin-sdk/tts-runtime` and plugin testing SDK surfaces to cover configured locators, agent-scope and persona `[]` clearing, and an existing stored config without the new field still synthesizing with its voice and model settings. HTTP responses and credentials are fixtures.
- Base/head regression proof for that exact test: copied unchanged into a temporary checkout at validation base `3270e6953055a12ceb9f8ad4a3dad908b5f27972`, it fails only the configured-locator assertion (3 pass, 1 fail); at head `c276373b2d091d6bd26c5c2f5e547dd8e09bd696`, all 4 tests pass.
- `pnpm run tsgo:extensions:test`, `pnpm run lint:plugins:no-extension-test-core-imports`, and `pnpm run test:extensions:package-boundary:compile` pass. The changed-test temp-directory report is clean.
- Live dictionary-backed `/tts audio` proof remains pending: the local ElevenLabs credential resolver returned unavailable, so no live vendor request was made. The human owner decision on the provider-local configuration option is also pending.
- Canonical contract: the [ElevenLabs TTS API](https://elevenlabs.io/docs/api-reference/text-to-speech/convert) accepts at most three pronunciation dictionary locators.

