## What Problem This Solves

OpenClaw's unused-export checks report 17 live declarations as dead because their consumers sit behind package-script generators, build and test-runner configuration, generated type declarations, emitted child source, or module-loader hooks that Knip does not follow on its own. That makes the cleanup signal unsafe: removing these declarations would break real build, validator, benchmark, fixture, or type contracts, while broad suppressions would hide genuinely unused exports.

## User Impact

There is no user-visible runtime change. Maintainers get accurate unused-export results for these real consumers while unrelated authored exports, production-only test seams, generated outputs, and strict production/full-tree/script boundaries remain detectable. No dependency, configuration option, schema, data, package API, runtime behavior, benchmark behavior, or migration changes.

## Why This Change Was Made

The repair teaches each existing owner about the consumer it already has:

- production analysis treats the maintained static-validator/runtime-asset generators and `tsdown.config.ts` as exact inputs;
- the script pass delegates parity and retention configuration to Knip's Vitest integration and projects the existing UI Vite import across the excluded workspace boundary;
- source-derived compiler edges model the exact names imported by generated benchmark child source and the team-report module hook, and derive the three runtime-manifest names from the live `tsdown.config.ts` import;
- Synology and the generated Presence declaration use ordinary top-level type imports so the real type contracts remain visible without an exception.

The compiler projections disappear when their source consumer disappears, and no file-, directory-, or export-family ignore was added.

