| `pnpm tsgo:core` | worktree setup refused shared external `node_modules`; exact-head clean-checkout CI is authoritative and pending |

### Current-main Bun CI repair

The first exact-head CI merge exposed a current-main test-fixture regression already present in #163039: under native Bun, `process.execPath` named Bun while an npm-diagnostics fixture asserted npm ownership. The production classifier was correct. This branch now uses the canonical real-Node test helper for that npm fixture; production behavior is unchanged. The full file passes 10/10 locally, and native-Bun CI is the authoritative cross-runtime check.

### Post-review safety correction

Exact-head review found a maximum-input priority inversion: a 1,024-character saved stop action plus a 255-character systemd unit could leave fresh recovery, verification, and responding-health lines as label-only ellipses. The new regression failed with `Recovery: ser…fe`, `Verification: ser…ng`, and `Health: res…e`. The shared cap owner now compacts the runtime headline and lower-priority reason text first, retaining the complete restart command, explicit safety states, omission marker, and saved-action tail within 1,500 characters.

A follow-up review found moderate pressure could trigger the same headline/reason compaction even when the final protected report still had 492 characters free. The shared cap owner now tries the full protected projection first and degrades only when that final projection actually exceeds the cap; the new 900-character-advice regression preserves the explanatory failure headline and exact reason code.

## Scope and code size

Against the branch merge base after conflict resolution: production +336 net lines; tests +718 net lines. The production growth is the generic bounded allocator plus explicit recovery/verification precedence needed to preserve safety facts and full operator commands within a hard cap. It replaces the arbitrary end-cut fallback rather than adding a second report path. No public API, config, schema, security, auth, or service behavior changes.

